Tree structure of the dataset:

14 Different Scans (sessions across 7 days)
  - Scan 1:
      - Muliple Imaging Planes
            - Plane 1
                  - ROI1 -> Nueron A (time series of activity)
                  - ROI2 -> Neuron B
            - Plane 2
                  - Ton of different Neurons (ROI's)
            - Plane 3
            - Plane 4
      - Visual Stimuli Shown during this scan
            - Cinematic
            - Sports1sm
            - POV movement
            - Monet2
            - Trippy
    - Scan 2:
        - Different Planes (not overlapping with Scan 1)
            - Different ROI's
    - Scan 3:
    - Scan 4: 
    - ..etc

RoiResponse1...10 just contain a ton of ROI's in them so you can pick 200 rois (neurons)
- RoiResponse contains a 3D volume so it scaned multple depths (layer 2/3 or layer4) so the 200 neurons can be anywhere in 
  a certain depth but they are from the same brain area (ie VISp)

Options to scale up decoding:

1. Decode across all planes (within one nwb file)
    Loop through all RoiResponseSeries in the same NWB file to analyze different brain locations
    Can answer which brain layer decods stimuli best, do higher visual areas show better/different decoding, how many neurons do you need from each plane
2. Decode across multiple sessions (different nwb files)
    Different populations of neurons were imaged in different sessions spanning different days
    with each neuron ROI uniquely determed by session, scan index, and ROI unit ID.
    Can answer can a decoder trained on session 1 predict stimuli in session 2?
    Are the same neuron types/located selected across sessions?
    Does pooling neuorns from multiple sessions improve decoding?

    Potential Problems:
      - Within one RoiResponseSeries all neurons saw the same stimulus presentations, all roireseponse
        in the same NWB file saw the same stimuli simultaneously so you can compare across planes
      - If you used different NWB files there are different clips


